on 4/2/2000</font><p></p>

<!-- Story -->
<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="black">
<b>General</b><br>
<b>1.0)</b> <A class=post href="#1.0" >What is this FAQ?</a><br>
<b>1.1)</b> <A class=post href="#1.1" >Who is making UO2?</a><br>
<b>1.2)</b> <A class=post href="#1.2" >What will the camera view by in UO2's 3d world?</a><br>
<b>1.3)</b> <A class=post href="#1.3" >What level of 3d graphics can we expect from UO2?</a><br>
<b>1.4)</b> <A class=post href="#1.4" >Will there be character classes in UO?</a><br>
<b>1.5)</b> <A class=post href="#1.5" >How many races will there be and what are they?</a><br>
<b>1.6)</b> <A class=post href="#1.6" >Will the plot be player driven?</a><br>
<b>1.7)</b> <A class=post href="#1.7" >When is UO2 due out?</a><br>
<b>1.8)</b> <A class=post href="#1.8" >Will there be unique names?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>UPDATED</b></font><br>
<b>1.9)</b> <A class=post href="#1.9" >Will UO2 have such interface features as last target?</a><br>
<b>1.10)</b> <A class=post href="#1.10" >Will UO2 have a mostly seamless map like UO or zones like EQ?</a><br>
<b>1.11)</b> <A class=post href="#1.11" >Is there going to be a beta test?</a><br>
<b>1.12)</b> <A class=post href="#1.12" >What is the Unofficial Question of the Week?</a><br>
<b>1.13)</b> <A class=post href="#1.13" >When does UO2 take place?</a><br>
<b>1.14)</b> <A class=post href="#1.14" >What about the prequel fiction?</a><br>
<b>1.15)</b> <A class=post href="#1.15" >Which PCGamer will feature UO2?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>1.16)</b> <A class=post href="#1.16" >How does UO2 look and feel compared to U9?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
<p>
<b>Gameplay</b><br>
<b>2.0)</b> <A class=post href="#2.0" >Will it be possible to play UO2 solo?</a><br>
<b>2.1)</b> <A class=post href="#2.1" >Will it be possible to make a Healer-Fighter or a Fighter-Mage in UO2?</a><br>
<b>2.2)</b> <A class=post href="#2.2" >Will the game reward teamwork and group adventures more than single player adventuring?</a><br>
<b>2.3)</b> <A class=post href="#2.3" >What if I want to just log on and lone wolf it. Can I still do that and have fun?</a><br>
<b>2.4)</b> <A class=post href="#2.4" >Well, what if I can't group with someone because they and I are too disparate in ability?</a><br>
<b>2.5)</b> <A class=post href="#2.5" >You mean that some skill trees will not be well suited for adventuring alone?</a><br>
<b>2.6)</b> <A class=post href="#2.6" >How will the idea of teleportation (recall and gate) be implemented in UO2?</a><br>
<b>2.7)</b> <A class=post href="#2.7" >How are you going to balance the game to be both easy to get into for the newbie and challenging for the weathered player?</a><br>
<b>2.8)</b> <A class=post href="#2.8" >What kind of weapons can I look forward to seeing?</a><br>
<b>2.9)</b> <A class=post href="#2.9" >Won't the technological race, the Jukas, have an huge advantage over other races in terms of weaponry?</a><br>
<b>2.10)</b> <A class=post href="#2.10" >Will there we aging in UO2?</a><br>
<b>2.11)</b> <A class=post href="#2.11" >What about realism like having to eat, drink and sleep?</a><br>
<b>2.12)</b> <A class=post href="#2.12" >How customizable is the character creation system in UO2?</a><br>
<b>2.13)</b> <A class=post href="#2.13" >What kind of quests can I look forward to?</a><br>
<b>2.14)</b> <A class=post href="#2.14" >What will magic be like in Britannia this time around?</a><br>
<b>2.15)</b> <A class=post href="#2.15" >How will the issue of pkillers be dealt with?</a><br>
<b>2.16)</b> <A class=post href="#2.16" >To set things straight, will there we sex in UO2?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.17)</b> <A class=post href="#2.17" >What exactly are skill trees and why have them?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.18)</b> <A class=post href="#2.18" >Why did the design team decide to not to use a use-based system?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.19)</b> <A class=post href="#2.19" >Can a craftsman gain skill points without killing things?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.20)</b> <A class=post href="#2.20" >Can I advance my blacksmithing skill by killing things?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.21)</b> <A class=post href="#2.21" >So my craftsman can sit on his fat hiney and advance in the safety of his own home?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.22)</b> <A class=post href="#2.22" >Will abilities gain with usage?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.23)</b> <A class=post href="#2.23" >Can you be a multiple grandmaster?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.24)</b> <A class=post href="#2.24" >Will there be a skill point cap?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.25)</b> <A class=post href="#2.25" >How do you guys feel about this advancement system you’ve concocted?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.26)</b> <A class=post href="#2.26" >Can you unlearn skills that you’ve learned?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>
<b>2.27)</b> <A class=post href="#2.27" >How will teleportation work in UO2?</a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
<p>
<a class=post name=1.0><b>1.0 What is this FAQ?</b></a><br>This FAQ is to serve as a supplement to the <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com/faq.html">Official FAQ</a>.  It will focus on comments made by the UO2 Development Team and other information not directly addressed in the Official FAQ.
<p>
<a class=post name=1.0><b>1.1 Who is making UO2?</b></a><br>UO2 is being made by Origin. Starr  Long, aka Blackthorn, is the producer of this latest persistant state from the creators of Ultima Online.  The official UO2 site may be found <a class=post href="http://www.uo2.com">here</a>.
<p>
<a class=post name=1.2><b>1.2 What will the camera view by in UO2's 3d world?
</b></a><br>"UO2 will feature a 'behind-the-head' cam, with some level of adjustable zoom. We are looking at other possible camera settings, including fully adjustable, 1st person, and UO-style isometric, but I cannot 100% guaruntee you at this time that we will solve all of the problems to make these viewpoints feel as smooth and as polished as the behind-cam, and publishing a stable and polished product will always be the UO2 team's number one priority.
<p>
I will say that playing the game right now with behind-cam is very fun, and allows for a nice range of peripheral vision while at the same time remaining immersive."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=1.3><b>1.3 What level of 3d graphics can we expect from UO2?
</b></a><br>"Garriott boasts, "UO2's (Ultima Online 2, scheduled to come out sometime this year) graphics are already better than Ultima 9's, and it's all in 3D. "-<a class=post href=http://www.auschron.com/issues/dispatch/2000-03-03/screens_feature7.html>Austin Chronicle Interview with RG</a>
<p>
<a class=post name=1.4><b>1.4 Will there be character classes in UO?
</b></a><br>No.  Ubiq said this:<blockquote>Somebody posted last week that it was 90% unlikely that the UO2 team would be using Character Classes. Let me go ahead and make that 100%. In my opinion, the ability to forge your own character along your own lines without being harshly limited by artificial class barriers is one of the true legacies of Ultima Online.<p>
We most likely will have 'professions', much like Ultima Online does, upon initial login, but this is primarily to help the newbie create a character quickly.</blockquote>

<a class=post name=1.5><b>1.5 How many races will there be and what are they?
</b></a><br>There will be 3 races, the humans, Meer and the enslaved Juka.  To learn more about these races, visit the Legends section of the <a class=post href=http://www.uo2.com>Official UO2 site</a>.
<p>
<a class=post name=1.6><b>1.6 Will the plot be player driven?
</b></a><br>"It's true that a plot needs interactivity, meaning that the players need to be able to actually have an effect on what happens whenever possible. Due to the way stories work, it's not always feasible to have every single facet of a story be open-ended. But Ahriman, our writer, is really excited about the prospect of creating dynamic stories, and finding new ways to involve the players on a personal level, giving people the chance to really affect the game.<p>
There's still a lot to be determined as to exactly how this should be done, but it's something that is important to us.
"-Cynthe
<p>
<a class=post name=1.7><b>1.7 When is UO2 due out?
</b></a><br>UO2 is scheduled to come out sometime this year-<a class=post href=http://www.auschron.com/issues/dispatch/2000-03-03/screens_feature7.html>Austin Chronicle Interview with RG</a>
<p>
<a class=post name=1.8><b>1.8 Will there be unique names?
</b></a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>UPDATED</b></font><br>Characters will have unique names in UO2. This should reduce exploits, make long-distance communication easier, and reduce customer service concerns overall. -Official FAQ
<p>
"Unique names are practically a requirement to ensuring that antisocial behavior is kept at a minimum, by (a) allowing community pressure to help prevent some anti-social behavior and by (b) making it easier for Tyrant and his GM staff to find potential troublemakers. 
<p>
In my 10 years of making and playing MUDs and MMPRPGs, I have seen all sorts of trouble that the lack of unique names can create, varying widely from the kind of scam that Sinij describes all the way to people engaging in character assassination schemes by pretending to be someone else and engaging in extremely nasty social behavior. End result: psychologically scarred victim, wrongfully accused and banned player, frazzled GM/player relations, etc. Anonymity is the enemy of an online GM support staff." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=1.9><b>1.9 Will UO2 have such interface features as last target?
</b></a><br>"The current plan is to have targetted spells be directed at your currently selected combat target, much like EQ or 'Last Target' in some of the UO Utility programs. We've been trying very hard to avoid adding various 'target modes', since they limit your mobility and have a potential to open up bugs and exploits. Also, it's very hard to click on a moving target reliably, especially when your camera is relatively mobile."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=1.10><b>1.10 Will UO2 have a mostly seamless map like UO or zones like EQ?
</b></a><br>UO2's map loading system, in this respect, will seem very similar to Ultima Online's. The vast majority of the world is designed to be seamless, except for dungeons, which are designed to be in a different 'zone'. 
 -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=1.11><b>1.11 Is there going to be a beta test?
</b></a><br>There will be a series of external tests starting in the near future. The testing positions available in the 1st phases of these tests will be open exclusively to current Ultima Online players. Please be sure to sign up on the mailing list and check back here often to stay updated. -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=1.12><b>1.12 What is the Unofficial Question of the Week?
</b></a><br>We want to put out the best game possible, and as a result, we welcome brainstorms and ideas from you, the fans. To facilitate this, once a week (barring crunch or other dev team time sinks), the UO2 team is choosing a fan 'dev' board with a healthy amount of traffic, and posting a question to invite responses from the fans. Players are strongly encouraged to offer their ideas and make their opinions known. Needless to say, there is no way that we can commit to using all (or any) of the ideas, but so far, this has been very successful in helping us to understand the interests and desires of our future playerbase! -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=1.13><b>1.13 When does UO2 take place?
</b></a><br>Ultima Online 2 is set approximately 200 years after a Great Cataclysm has ripped through the world of Ultima Online. Ultima Online is set in a divergent timeline, adrift from the timeline of the standalone Ultima games, diverging at the shattering of the gem of immortality at the end of Ultima 1. The events of Ultima Online are occurring at the same time that Ultima 4 and Ultima 5 were happening in the Primary Thread. -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=1.14><b>1.14 What about the prequel fiction?
</b></a><br>The prequel fiction for Ultima Online 2 chronicles the events that lead up to the Great Cataclysm. 
<p>
This means that the events of Ultima 2 through 9 have never happened in UO's Britannia, although people from Ultimas 2 through 9 (Blackthorn, Minax, Dupre, etc) are affecting the course of UO's history, albeit in different ways from the standalone games. For example, Lord Blackthorn was never corrupted by the Shadowlords, and thus has a friendly, yet adversarial, relationship with British in Ultima Online. -Official FAQ
<p>
Be sure to check out the prequel fiction on the <a class=post href=http://www.uo2.com>Official Site</a>.
<p>
<a class=post name=1.15><b>1.15 Which PCGamer will feature UO2? 
</b></a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>"Actually, it will be in the June issue, which should be available right around the same time as E3. My apologies if anything was said to implicate otherwise. :) 
<p>
It is coming, though! Waiting for a later issue enabled us to add newer screenshots, so I have faith that it was worth the wait. :) " -Cynthe
<p>
<a class=post name=1.16><b>1.16 How does UO2 look and feel compared to U9?
</b></a> <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font><br>"Ah... Well we have taken a similar approach to what we did with Ultima: Ascension, but with better learning. The world will ultimately have weather, day/night etc. As the world will be populated with real people we will have less issues with AI then we had in Ultima: Ascension. Yet still we have a much better plan for Ultima Online 2 in how NPC's and Creatures will operate. The world is also a much better designed 3D landscape and thus has better interactions than Ultima: Ascension with the environment itself. The interface is kinda cool too. Much more intuitive than before..." -Richard Garriott
<p>
<a class=post name=2.0><b>2.0 Will it be possible to play UO2 solo?</b></a><br>"Yes. If you have built a character with a skill set that is good for fighting alone."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.1><b>2.1 Will it be possible to make a Healer-Fighter or a Fighter-Mage in UO2?</b></a><br>"Yes. But he will never, ever be as good a healer as a dedicated healer, nor as good a fighter as a dedicated fighter (same with our tank mage). It is up to the player to decide whether or not to be a jack-of-all-trades (i.e. learn from another skill tree, and thus be more self-sufficient), or to specialize (and thus be more dependent on grouping, but to probably be invited into more groups and advance faster because of his improved healing abilities)."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.2><b>2.2 Will the game reward teamwork and group adventures more than single player adventuring?
</b></a><br>"Absolutely. We are spending a fair amount of our focus on the advancement system, the party system, and the monster AI to gear them against groups, to make the best possible group adventuring experience. We are a massively multiplayer game first. If you make friends, you will continue to play, thus enriching the community and paying my rent. As a result, we will take steps to encourage teamwork without enforcing it."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.3><b>2.3 What if I want to just log on and lone wolf it. Can I still do that and have fun?
</b></a><br>"Yes, assuming you've built your character in such a way that that's possible. It will be more difficult, it will be more expensive, and it will be slower going than adventuring in a nice, well-rounded party with support members."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.4><b>2.4 Well, what if I can't group with someone because they and I are too disparate in ability?
</b></a><br>"Our goal is to allow everyone to 'party' with everyone in UO2. Quite simply, I believe that a system that prevents you from adventuring with your friends is broken, and that if you are worried about twinking, you should take other paths to prevent that from occurring."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.5><b>2.5 You mean that some skill trees will not be well suited for adventuring alone?
</b></a><br>"Of course. Some skill trees are not well suited for adventuring at all! Examples include Tailoring and Blacksmithing. However, skill trees such as the healing-oriented school of magic are designed primarily to perform a support role."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.6><b>2.6 How will the idea of teleportation (recall and gate) be implemented in UO2?
</b></a><br>"We have a solution in mind, it allows players to get around to some degree, but does not offer the total freedom of UO's recall and gate. We want to be able to talk with you guys about recall in more details to flesh out these plans in the near future without distraction from other topics, look for it in a QotW in the near future."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.7><b>2.7 How are you going to balance the game to be both easy to get into for the newbie and challenging for the weathered player?
</b></a><br>Ubiq had this to say about his philosophy on how to get into the game:
<blockquote>The combat system should be ridiculously easy for a newbie to engage in and take part in with confidence.</blockquote>But also:<blockquote>The combat system should reward the high-level warrior. Right now, when you watch a wizard in most MMPRPGs, you say 'wow, I wish I could do that'. We want the newbies to say the same thing for warriors.</blockquote>
He then added this comment to bolster his original position:
<blockquote>I didn't say that the combat system should be ridiculously easy for a grandmaster, just for a newbie. The best games are easy to learn, but difficult to master (examples: chess, go, Magic). We are working hard to make the game inviting for the new player, while at the same time giving you die-hard roleplayers the complexity you crave at the higher levels.</blockquote>
<a class=post name=2.8><b>2.8 What kind of weapons can I look forward to seeing?
</b></a><br>"You're going to see a LOT of things you haven't seen before in a MMPRPG, weapons-wise. You have been warned."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.9><b>2.9 Won't the technological race, the Jukas, have an huge advantage over other races in terms of weaponry?
</b></a><br>"To say that the Technologists' weapons in UO2 will be far beyond today's standards is to imply that we are on the same technological path.<p>
Interesting historical tidbit: when the Europeans landed in South America several centuries ago, they were shocked to find the Incas, a civilization that technologically surpassed their own in many, many ways, and yet had never stumbled upon the wheel."-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.10><b>2.10 Will there we aging in UO2?
</b></a><br>"We talked a bit about aging here in UO2 central. The problem with a feature like aging is that it punishes your most loyal customers, who also tend to be the pillars of your community. I have seen aging work in a number of MUDs, and doing something like selling 'anti-aging' potions is actually a fairly useful money sink. On the other hand, that's coding and development time that could be spent on adding a feature that adds value to your character instead. =)"-Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.11><b>2.11 What about realism like having to eat, drink and sleep?
</b></a><br>The Official FAQ states:
<blockquote>Food, drink, and rest will not be necessary to survive in Ultima Online 2 in that you will not die simply from hunger. However, the UO2 design team feels that incorporating elements of food and rest helps enhance the gameplay, and so you can expect to see some benefits from being well-fed and well-rested. </blockquote>
Off the record Ubiq stated:
<blockquote>...all forms of entertainment (including online games) need to look at "fun" and "realism" as separate things. They are not necessarily the same. It might be realistic for people to do something (like sleep for 8 hours) but that doesn't make for compelling entertainment.</blockquote>
<a class=post name=2.12><b>2.12 How customizable is the character creation system in UO2?
</b></a><br>"From the FAQ: "Our goal is to match the level of customization evident in Ultima Online, with the ability to mix and match different hair styles, skin tones and to have multiple layers of clothing of different hues. This is a lofty goal and an incredible technical challenge, but we think we can get close to it, if not achieve it."
<p>
Basically, we agree that making your character look as unique as possible is one of the best ways to really get into the game, and that's something we really want to see happen. We don't currently have plans to offer multiple body shapes, but I'll happily pass your suggestion on to the UO2 dev team!"-Cynthe
<p>
<a class=post name=2.13><b>2.13 What kind of quests can I look forward to?
</b></a><br>UO2 will feature a robust 'quest engine', which will provide exciting and interesting things to do for all types of players. The culmination of these quests is the Path of the Avatar, where players can choose to live by and enforce the eight Virtues from classic Ultima - or they can deny the Virtues and walk the path of evil. Between that, the endless number of events, and a world full of tense political intrigue, you’ll never be without something fun and exciting to do in the game. -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=2.14><b>2.14 What will magic be like in Britannia this time around?
</b></a><br>To some degree. UO2's magic system will be broken up into a series of magic schools. Players will have the option of mixing and matching schools, or focusing on one school to master it. Most of the spells from UO will be making a comeback, but we plan to include many more in order to populate the schools of magic. Expect to see many new and interesting spells in UO2. -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=2.15><b>2.15 How will the issue of pkillers be dealt with?
</b></a><br>Player killing will be limited through the use of Player vs. Player zones. This will give players a chance to develop their characters and adventure without risk, and yet still give the adrenaline rush of player-vs-player combat once they have determined they are ready to face higher risks for greater rewards. -Official FAQ
<p>
<a class=post name=2.16><b>2.16 To set things straight, will there we sex in UO2? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"...according to Ubiq, our lead designer, sex won't be in UO2 any more than it is in UO." -Cynthe
<p>
<a class=post name=2.17><b>2.17 What exactly are skill trees and why have them?? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Skill trees start from a small number (currently about a dozen) boxes, and expand and unfold quickly as you advance. Fully expanded, our skill trees are large enough and numerous enough to wallpaper a room - I know this because Mobius has done so. Each skill tree has numerous branches, which offer different sub abilities. Some skill trees are quite large (the combat one currently has 150+ skills in it), whereas others are quite small (there are two skill trees that only have two boxes in them). We're talking a lot of freakin' skills here.
<p>
It is difficult enough to simply implement all of the ways that these skills can function, advance, and interact. Ensuring that each skill can only advance on use of that skill quickly becomes a technical infeasibility, a data storage monstrosity and a design nightmare. It also becomes difficult to add new things, and hard to balance. Given the options, we had a couple of choices: <br>
* Reduce the number of cool skills that players can learn and perform, but allow them to advance only by doing them.<br>
* Put in a simple-to-use, hard-to-exploit advancement system, and then put in a ton of freakin' skills.
<p>
Now then, if you ask me what makes for a better game, a more immersive world, and a more complete community, it is one that offers more skills and ways to grow and expand those skills, and by extension, greater character customization. 
" -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.18><b>2.18 Why did the design team decide to not to use a use-based system? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Having implemented two use-based systems in the past, and then observed Ultima Online’s experiences, I have come to the point where I do not like use-based systems. I realize that this is a matter of philosophy, and you are welcome to disagree, but then there you go. 
<p>
Probably the largest reason I don’t like use-based systems is that use-based systems promote highly unrealistic behavior, when the opposite is the goal of the system. 
<p>
Other problems with use-based systems: they reward the mundane instead of the momentous. Advancement should feel rewarding. You shouldn’t think ‘If I macro this, my character will go up a level while I’m on the can.’. The journey should be part of the reward. 
<p>
Use-based systems tend to distort the economy. They tend to flood the market with goods that no one needs or wants, which also causes lag and database delays. They tend to make skills that require materials and reagents ungodly expensive. They tend to favor the hardcore gamer disproportionately.
<p>
Lastly, use-based systems are very, very difficult to code, and very, very difficult to balance. They are incredibly difficult to make exploit-proof. They are even more difficult to code when you try to solve all of the above problems. 
<p>
Does that mean that use-based systems are bad, and the game will fail if you don’t have them? Of course not. Ultima Online has one, and it serves that game very, very well. Use-cased systems have several advantages that cannot be discounted. However, the UO2 team felt that it was worth trying something new and innovative, in hopes that we could create a system best suited for the gaming experience we have in mind." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.19><b>2.19 Can a craftsman gain skill points without killing things? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Yes. We have several plans for this and are looking at the best option now." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.20><b>2.20 Can I advance my blacksmithing skill by killing things? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Yes. The pros far outweigh the cons on this one." -Ubiq
<p>
However...
<p>"If you are an accomplished blacksmith, and you attempt to advance by killing things, you will find it inefficient, since you're not very good at killing things. You're far better off doing what you're good at, which is makin' swords. " -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.21><b>2.21 So my craftsman can sit on his fat hiney and advance in the safety of his own home? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"I didn’t say that. When designing this system, we will almost certainly consider the cost/risk/reward ratios, to come up with something that we think is fair." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.22><b>2.22 Will abilities gain with usage? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Not directly. Indirectly, usage will result in successful advancement, which will result in more skill points, which will result in advancement of abilities." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.23><b>2.23 Can you be a multiple grandmaster? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Yes – in most things. Some skills, such as flaming kitten juggling, will be relatively easy to grandmaster, since that particular skill tree, if it actually existed, would be quite stumpy and shrublike. Other skills, such as the advanced combat and magery skills require an incredible level of dedication to simply SINGLE GM them. Attempts to get a second GM on the side for these will be very, very difficult, and will be limited to either (a) aforementioned shrub-like trees and (b) other skills that share common prereqs (i.e. a mage might be able to pick up a second school of magic, a martial artist might learn axe-fighting). 
<p>
My goal is no dual GM tankmages. If a way to develop one pops up, I'll whack it down like I'm playin' whackamole. 
" -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.24><b>2.24 Will there be a skill point cap? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"At launch, there will most likely be a cap. We don’t think one is necessary, but we felt that putting in a cap initially would protect us just in case some advancement bug or exploit crept past us." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.25><b>2.25 How do you guys feel about this advancement system you’ve concocted? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"We actually think that it is one of the most solid, well-thought out parts of UO2’s design, and at this stage we feel like it’s the best total advancement system that we’ve ever seen in a MMPRPG." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.26><b>2.26 Can you unlearn skills that you’ve learned? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>"Yes, provided the skill is not a prerequisite for another skill. The rate that you can ‘unlearn’ a skill is controlled, to prevent a blacksmith from becoming a warrior overnight." -Ubiq
<p>
<a class=post name=2.27><b>2.27 How will teleportation work in UO2? <font face="Arial" color="Blue"><b>NEW</b></font>
</b></a><br>We don't know yet, as it was even a Question of the Week but we do know this bit of information:<br>"First off, I will confess that moongates are currently planned to be a core part of UO2's teleportation philosophy. How exactly they will work may differ from UO, however. " -Ubiq
</font>	

